[R0243] year=2026 | venue=2026 Silicon Valley Cybersecurity Conference, SVCC 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Autonomous Detection Engineering: From Threat Research to Detection Authoring
KEYWORDS: agentic AI; Autonomous detection engineering; large language models; threat intelligence; Defects; Guard rails; Libraries; Network security; Open source software; Open systems; Query languages; Security systems; Agentic AI; Autonomous detection; Autonomous detection engineering; Coverage gaps; Engineering workflows; Feedback loops; Language model; Large language model; Security operation center; Threat intelligence; Codes (symbols)
ABSTRACT: How can Security Operations Centers keep pace with adversary tradecraft when threats evolve faster than analysts can write detections? Today, most detection engineering workflows remain fragmented: threat researchers manually dissect intelligence reports, analysts track coverage gaps across disparate out-of-the-box detections, and detection authors hand-code logic across siloed repositories with limited feedback on quality or overlap. This talk presents a practical, end-to-end pipeline that unifies these stages-closing the loop from raw threat intelligence to validated, production-ready detection code. We will begin with the upstream challenge: turning unstructured threat reports and open-source threat intelligence feeds into structured, actionable intelligence. We describe an agentic approach that automatically extracts Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTPs) from heterogeneous sources-reports, web content, breach documents-and maps them to the MITRE ATT&CK framework. The agent then performs detection coverage analysis through vector similarity search and largelanguage-model (LLM) validation, surfacing prioritized gaps across detection libraries in minutes rather than the days or weeks required by manual triage. We will then transition to the downstream problem of detection authoring at scale. We present an agentic, Model Context Protocol (MCP)-driven workflow that scaffolds new detections from the identified coverage gaps, validates syntax against target query languages, eliminates duplicates through semantic similarity checks, and ships production code with built-in quality guardrails-all within familiar developer tooling. We evaluate multiple authoring paradigms and share measurable improvements in detection quality, coverage lift, and engineering velocity. We will also discuss the feedback loop that connects deployed detections back to the coverage model, enabling continuous measurement of an organization's defensive posture over time. We will conclude with practical advice on applying AI to security problems at scale. We discuss how to select the right level of autonomy for each task and where human-in-the-loop oversight remains essential to maintain trust and correctness. We share strategies for measuring detection quality beyond simple metrics. Finally, we reflect on common failure modes observed in practice-such as ungrounded or hallucinated logic and over-reliance on plausible outputs without verification- and how disciplined grounding, evaluation, and feedback loops can mitigate these risks in production systems. © 2026 IEEE.
